  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) achieves 48.5% accuracy, establishing RNA as the dominant modality, while config_010 (image_only, resnet50) is very weak at 20.8%. The key uncertainty is whether moving from a single profile to multiple profiles with aggregation can improve the RNA signal, since this structural choice gates a large portion of the remaining search space (all multiprofile and multimodal configs). Question: config_002 (rna_only, multiprofile, pca_n2000, avgpool) is the minimal change from config_001—same encoder, same modality—adding only multiprofile with a simple avgpool aggregation. This cleanly isolates the effect of profile setting/aggregation. Why this is the best next experiment: if multiprofile+avgpool improves over singleprofile, it validates the multiprofile branch and justifies investing in aggregation variants (transformer) and learned encoders on top of it; if it does not help, we can prune the multiprofile branch and focus budget on encoder choice (scvi, scgpt) or better image encoders. This single experiment reduces the most consequential uncertainty in the search tree with a low-risk, well-controlled comparison."
}

